ers, but that's not possible.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the player should discard a tile that is not useful for any melds, like B4. Since B4 is a single tile and not part of any chow, discarding it would leave the hand with only Characters and Bamboos, which might be easier to form melds. For example, after discarding B4, the hand is all Characters and Bamboos. If the player can form two chows in Bamboos and one in Characters, but again, no.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the player should discard a tile that is part of a potential chow. For example, if they discard T9, maybe they can form a chow of T7-T8-T9 if they draw a T9. But the current hand has T7 and T8. If they discard T9, then drawing a T9 would give them a pair and a chow. But that's just one chow.\n\nAlternatively, if they discard T7, they can form a chow of T6-T7-T8 if they draw a T7. But again, only one chow.\n\nHmm. Given that the current hand is not a winning hand, the player needs to discard a tile and wait for a draw. The key is to discard a tile that is least useful for forming melds, so that when drawing, there are more possibilities.\n\nLooking at the tiles in the hand:\n\n- W4: part of possible chow with W5, W6 (but no W5)\n- W6: same\n- W9: terminal, not part of chows\n- T1: part of T1-T2-T3 chow (but no T2 or T3)\n- T6: part of T6-T7-T8 chow\n- T7: part of T6-T7-T8 chow\n- T8: part of T6-T7-T8 chow\n- T9: part of T7-T8-T9 chow (but no T7 or T8 in the other parts)\n- B4: single tile\n\nSo B4 is the least useful for forming melds. Discarding B4 would leave the hand with only Characters and Bamboos, which might be better for forming chows. So discarding B4 could be a good move. Alternatively, discarding T9 or W9, which are terminals, might also be good.\n\nIf the player discards B4, then when they draw a tile, they can try to form a hand with all Characters and Bamboos. For example, if they draw a T2, they can form a chow of T1-T2-T3 (but they have T1x2, so maybe not). Alternatively, if they draw a T5, it's not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, if they discard T9, they can form a chow of T7-T8-T9 if they draw a T9. But since they have T7 and T8, discarding T9 would allow that.\n\nBut since the unshown tiles include T9:3, there are still three T9s left. So discarding T9 might be safe.\n\nAlternatively, discarding W9 (which is a terminal) might be better, as terminals are less useful for chows.\n\nAnother angle: the player's Seat Wind is West, and the Prevailing Wind is South. So if they can form a Seat Wind or Prevailing Wind pung, that would add points. But they don't have any wind tiles in their hand.\n\nThe player's hand has no wind or dragon tiles, so the only way to get points is through suited tiles.\n\nConsidering that, the best bet is to discard a tile that's not part of any potential chow. B4 is such a tile. So discarding B4 would be the best move, as it leaves the hand with more potential for forming chows in the future.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [Discarding B4 allows the hand to focus on forming chows in Characters and Bamboos, maximizing flexibility for future draws.]\nReason: [B4 is a single tile with no potential for forming chows or pungs. Removing it leaves the hand with more suited tiles that can form chows, especially in the Bamboos (T6-T7-T8). This increases the chance of completing a valid hand with chows and pairs.]\nAnswer: Play B4"}
