d T8, they still have W4, W6, W8. So same possibilities. So the choice between T8 and W4. Which one to discard?\n\nLooking at the unshown tiles, T8 has 1 left, so if they discard T8, the chance of drawing it is lower than W4, which has 2 left. So maybe it's better to discard W4, keeping T8 in hand. Because if they discard T8, they might lose a potential tile that's rare (only one left). But if they discard W4, they still have two left, so it's more likely to be drawn.\n\nTherefore, the better choice is to discard W4. So the valid action would be Play W4.\n\nWait, but the user's valid actions include Play W4. Let me check again.\n\nIf they discard W4, their hand becomes [W6, W8, T8, B5]. They can wait for W7 or B5. The unshown B5 has 2 left, W7 has 3 left. That's good. So this seems like a solid move.\n\nAlternatively, if they discard T8, their hand is [W4, W6, W8, B5], same possibilities. But T8 has only one left, so maybe keeping T8 is better. So maybe discard W4.\n\nAnother angle: the user's hand has a pair of B5 if they can draw another. If they discard B5, they lose that chance. So it's better to keep B5. So the best options are to discard W4, T8, or W6. Between those, discarding W4 or T8.\n\nConsidering the unshown tiles, W4 has 2 left, T8 has 1 left. So discarding W4 is better because it's more available. So the answer is Play W4.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [Discarding W4 leaves the hand with potential to form a chow with W7 or a pair with B5, maximizing winning chances.]\nReason: [By discarding W4, the hand retains W6, W8, T8, and B5. This allows waiting for W7 to complete the chow W6-W7-W8 or another B5 to form a pair. B5 has 2 remaining in unshown tiles, and W7 has 3, offering multiple winning paths. Discarding W4 prioritizes flexibility over less-critical tiles.]\nAnswer: Play W4"}
